                <pb facs="1275" n="1224" sameAs="frus:frus1977-80v15/pg1224"/>


                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#bold">Paper Prepared in the Department of State</hi><anchor
                        n="5" xml:id="d470-f1"/>
                </head>
                <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                    <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                        ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-content-and-sibling-dates"
                        calendar="gregorian" notAfter="1979-06-13T23:59:59-04:00"
                        notBefore="1979-06-12T00:00:00-04:00">undated</date></dateline>
                <p rendition="#section-heading #italic">U.S.
                    OBJECTIVES TOWARD CENTRAL AMERICA</p>
                <p>—movement toward more open political systems, free elections, and democratic
                    processes;</p>
                <p>—reduction of violence and instability;</p>
                <p>—strengthening of centrist and moderate groups and the linkages among them;</p>
                <p>—a diminution in the appeal and power of radical elements and Castro-linked groups;</p>
                <p>—economic and social development which would improve the services and
                    opportunities available to the people and reduce the vulnerabilities and
                    frustrations that lead to political tensions;</p>
                <p>—renewed momentum toward greater regional economic cooperation and integration as
                    an effective framework to foster modernization and reduce intra-regional
                    tensions, including demographic problems;</p>
                <p>—better observance of human rights, an end to terrorism from both left and right,
                    and a cessation of such practices as political assassination and torture;</p>
                <p>—resolution of the area’s international conflicts, especially the Honduras—El
                    Salvador dispute; and</p>
                <p>—a friendly perception of the U.S. and
                    willingness to cooperate with us for larger goals, including strategic
                    considerations related to both the Panama Canal and the hemisphere’s
                    security.</p>



                <xi:include href="d470-a1-a1.xml" xpointer="d470-a1-a1"/>
